  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none; the full paper text is present.",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper measures safety changes caused by general pruning and its own neuron-restoration method, not the difference-in-means residual direction or an intervention on that direction. It cites Arditi et al. in Appendix D but does not rely on or test their direction as a method.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports harmful-request safety results after pruning and HSR restoration. These bear on safety behavior but do not test any listed version of the single-direction claim. Its brief discussion of the refusal direction in Appendix D refers to prior work; it is not a result of this study.",
    "test_reason": "The study prunes LVLMs and LLMs (Wanda, SNIP, SparseGPT) and restores safety-critical neurons in selected attention heads (HSR). It never computes, ablates, adds or edits along a difference-in-means refusal direction. Arditi et al. is mentioned only in Appendix E, as a qualitative comparison to the authors' layer-wise Jaccard overlap analysis. Its results are pruning-induced ASR changes, which bear on no listed version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record's paraphrase is correct. The authors find that pruning degrades LVLM safety. HSR restores safety-critical neurons within safety-critical heads and recovers part of the lost safety with minimal utility cost. Recovery is weaker for Llama3-based models. A small subset of neurons matters disproportionately for safety, and some neurons harm it.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "HSR ranks attention heads and neurons using safety and utility data, then restores pruned neurons; it does not derive or ablate a harmful-minus-harmless residual-stream direction. The paper cites the original direction work only as related prior work.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Reported outcomes vary with pruning method, model, sparsity, and HSR hyperparameters; the paper states that recovery is weaker for Llama3-based LVLMs and greatest at 50% sparsity in its sparsity comparison.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Unstructured and 2:4 pruning raised harmful-request ASR in all six LVLMs and two LLMs. Restoring a small set of safety-critical neurons in top safety heads recovered roughly 14–104% of the lost safety.",
        "proposed_claim": "Safety-relevant weights are sparse and are preferentially removed by utility-based pruning; restoring a tiny fraction of them partly restores refusal of harmful requests.",
